⟦P0⟧Go Vulnerability Database⟦P1⟧⟦P2⟧Back to Go Vulnerability Management⟦P3⟧⟦P4⟧Overview⟦P5⟧The Go vulnerability database (⟦P6⟧⟦P7⟧⟦P8⟧)⟦P9⟧serves Go vulnerability information in the⟦P10⟧⟦P11⟧Open Source Vulnerability (⟦P12⟧) schema⟦P13⟧.⟦P14⟧You can also browse vulnerabilities in the database at ⟦P15⟧⟦P16⟧⟦P17⟧.⟦P18⟧⟦P19⟧Do not⟦P20⟧ rely on the contents of the ⟦P21⟧ Git repository. The ⟦P22⟧ files in that⟦P23⟧repository are maintained using an internal format that may change⟦P24⟧without warning.⟦P25⟧Contributing⟦P26⟧We would love for all Go package maintainers to ⟦P27⟧contribute⟦P28⟧⟦P29⟧information about public vulnerabilities in their own projects,⟦P30⟧and ⟦P31⟧update⟦P32⟧ existing information about vulnerabilities⟦P33⟧in their Go packages.⟦P34⟧We aim to make reporting a low friction process,⟦P35⟧so feel free to ⟦P36⟧send us your suggestions⟦P37⟧.⟦P38⟧Please ⟦P39⟧do not⟦P40⟧ use the forms above to report a vulnerability in the Go⟦P41⟧standard library or sub-repositories.⟦P42⟧Instead, follow the process at ⟦P43⟧⟦P44⟧⟦P45⟧⟦P46⟧for vulnerabilities about the Go project.⟦P47⟧The canonical Go vulnerability database, ⟦P48⟧⟦P49⟧⟦P50⟧,⟦P51⟧is an ⟦P52⟧ server that can respond to ⟦P53⟧ requests for the endpoints specified below.⟦P54⟧The endpoints have no query parameters, and no specific headers are required.⟦P55⟧Because of this, even a site serving from a fixed file system (including a ⟦P56⟧ ⟦P57⟧)⟦P58⟧can implement this ⟦P59⟧.⟦P60⟧Each endpoint returns a JSON-encoded response, in either uncompressed⟦P61⟧(if requested as ⟦P62⟧) or gzipped form (if requested as ⟦P63⟧).⟦P64⟧The endpoints are:⟦P65⟧Returns metadata about the database:⟦P66⟧Note that the modified time ⟦P67⟧should not⟦P68⟧ be compared to wall clock time,⟦P69⟧⟦P70⟧. for purposes of cache invalidation, as there may a delay in making⟦P71⟧database modifications live.⟦P72⟧See ⟦P73⟧⟦P74⟧⟦P75⟧ for a live example.⟦P76⟧Returns a list containing metadata about each module in the database:⟦P77⟧See ⟦P78⟧⟦P79⟧⟦P80⟧ for a live example.⟦P81⟧Returns a list containing metadata about each vulnerability in the database:⟦P82⟧See ⟦P83⟧⟦P84⟧⟦P85⟧ for a live example.⟦P86⟧Returns the individual report for the vulnerability with ⟦P87⟧ ⟦P88⟧,⟦P89⟧in ⟦P90⟧ format (described below in ⟦P91⟧Schema⟦P92⟧).⟦P93⟧See ⟦P94⟧⟦P95⟧⟦P96⟧⟦P97⟧for a live example.⟦P98⟧Bulk download⟦P99⟧To make it easier to download the entire Go vulnerability database,⟦P100⟧a zip file containing all the index and ⟦P101⟧ files is available at⟦P102⟧⟦P103⟧⟦P104⟧⟦P105⟧.⟦P106⟧Usage in ⟦P107⟧⟦P108⟧By default, ⟦P109⟧ uses the canonical Go vulnerability database at ⟦P110⟧⟦P111⟧⟦P112⟧.⟦P113⟧The command can be configured to contact a different vulnerability database using the ⟦P114⟧ flag,which accepts a vulnerability database ⟦P115⟧ with protocol ⟦P116⟧, ⟦P117⟧, or ⟦P118⟧.⟦P119⟧To work correctly with ⟦P120⟧, the vulnerability database specified must implement the ⟦P121⟧ described above. The ⟦P122⟧ command uses compressed ".⟦P123⟧" endpoints when reading from an http(s) source, and the ".json" endpoints when reading from a file source.⟦P124⟧Legacy ⟦P125⟧⟦P126⟧The canonical database contains some additional endpoints that are part of a legacy ⟦P127⟧.⟦P128⟧We plan to remove support for these endpoints soon. If you are relying on the legacy ⟦P129⟧⟦P130⟧and need additional time to migrate, ⟦P131⟧please let us know⟦P132⟧.⟦P133⟧Schema⟦P134⟧Reports use the⟦P135⟧⟦P136⟧Open Source Vulnerability (⟦P137⟧) schema⟦P138⟧.⟦P139⟧The Go vulnerability database assigns the following meanings to the fields:⟦P140⟧id⟦P141⟧The id field is a unique identifier for the vulnerability entry. It is a string⟦P142⟧of the format ⟦P143⟧⟦P144⟧⟦P145⟧>-⟦P146⟧⟦P147⟧>.⟦P148⟧affected⟦P149⟧The ⟦P150⟧affected⟦P151⟧ field is a⟦P152⟧⟦P153⟧ array containing objects that describes the module versions that contain⟦P154⟧the vulnerability.⟦P155⟧affected⟦P156⟧⟦P157⟧.package⟦P158⟧The⟦P159⟧⟦P160⟧affected⟦P161⟧⟦P162⟧.package⟦P163⟧⟦P164⟧field is a ⟦P165⟧ object identifying the affected ⟦P166⟧module.⟦P167⟧ The object has two⟦P168⟧required fields:⟦P169⟧⟦P170⟧ecosystem⟦P171⟧: this will always be "Go"⟦P172⟧⟦P173⟧name⟦P174⟧: this is the Go module path⟦P175⟧Importable packages in the standard library will have the name ⟦P176⟧stdlib⟦P177⟧.⟦P178⟧The go command will have the name ⟦P179⟧toolchain⟦P180⟧.⟦P181⟧affected⟦P182⟧⟦P183⟧.⟦P184⟧⟦P185⟧The⟦P186⟧⟦P187⟧affected⟦P188⟧⟦P189⟧.⟦P190⟧⟦P191⟧⟦P192⟧field is a ⟦P193⟧ object with additional information about the vulnerability,⟦P194⟧which is used by Go's vulnerability detection tools.⟦P195⟧For now, ecosystem specific will always be an object with a single field,⟦P196⟧⟦P197⟧.⟦P198⟧affected⟦P199⟧⟦P200⟧.⟦P201⟧⟦P202⟧The ⟦P203⟧ field is a ⟦P204⟧ array containing⟦P205⟧the packages and symbols affected by the vulnerability. Each object in the⟦P206⟧array will have these two fields:⟦P207⟧⟦P208⟧path:⟦P209⟧ a string with the import path of the package containing the vulnerability⟦P210⟧⟦P211⟧symbols:⟦P212⟧ a string array with the names of the symbols (function or method) that contains the vulnerability⟦P213⟧⟦P214⟧goos⟦P215⟧: a string array with the execution operating system where the symbols appear, if known⟦P216⟧⟦P217⟧goarch⟦P218⟧: a string array with the architecture where the symbols appear, if known⟦P219⟧The ⟦P220⟧ field contains custom fields specific to the Go vulnerability database.⟦P221⟧The ⟦P222⟧ field is a string representing the fully-qualified⟦P223⟧⟦P224⟧ of the Go vulnerability report, ⟦P225⟧, "⟦P226⟧".⟦P227⟧The ⟦P228⟧ field is a string representing the review⟦P229⟧status of the vulnerability report. If not present, the report should be⟦P230⟧considered ⟦P231⟧. The possible values are:⟦P232⟧⟦P233⟧: The report was automatically generated based on another source, such as⟦P234⟧a ⟦P235⟧ or ⟦P236⟧. Its data may be limited and has not been verified by the Go team.⟦P237⟧⟦P238⟧: The report originated from the Go team, or was generated based on an external source.⟦P239⟧A member of the Go team has reviewed the report, and where appropriate, added additional data.⟦P240⟧For information on other fields in the schema, refer to the ⟦P241⟧⟦P242⟧ spec⟦P243⟧.⟦P244⟧Note on Versions⟦P245⟧Our tooling attempts to automatically map modules and versions in⟦P246⟧source advisories to canonical Go modules and versions, in accordance with⟦P247⟧standard ⟦P248⟧Go module version numbers⟦P249⟧. Tools like⟦P250⟧⟦P251⟧ are designed to rely on these standard versions to determine⟦P252⟧whether a Go project is affected by a vulnerability in a dependency or not.⟦P253⟧In some cases, such as when a Go project uses its own versioning scheme,⟦P254⟧the mapping to standard Go versions can fail. When this happens, the Go⟦P255⟧vulnerability database report may conservatively list all Go versions as⟦P256⟧affected. This ensures that tools such as ⟦P257⟧ do not fail to report⟦P258⟧vulnerabilities due to unrecognized version ranges (false negatives).⟦P259⟧However, conservatively listing all versions as affected may cause tools to⟦P260⟧incorrectly report a fixed version of a module as containing the vulnerability⟦P261⟧(false positives).⟦P262⟧If you believe ⟦P263⟧ is incorrectly reporting (or failing to report) a⟦P264⟧vulnerability, please⟦P265⟧⟦P266⟧suggest an edit⟦P267⟧⟦P268⟧to the vulnerability report and we will review it.⟦P269⟧Examples⟦P270⟧All vulnerabilities in the Go vulnerability database use the ⟦P271⟧ schema⟦P272⟧described above.⟦P273⟧See the links below for examples of different Go vulnerabilities:⟦P274⟧⟦P275⟧Go standard library vulnerability⟦P276⟧ (⟦P277⟧):⟦P278⟧⟦P279⟧⟦P280⟧⟦P281⟧,⟦P282⟧⟦P283⟧⟦P284⟧⟦P285⟧⟦P286⟧⟦P287⟧Go toolchain vulnerability⟦P288⟧ (⟦P289⟧):⟦P290⟧⟦P291⟧⟦P292⟧⟦P293⟧,⟦P294⟧⟦P295⟧⟦P296⟧⟦P297⟧⟦P298⟧⟦P299⟧Vulnerability in Go module⟦P300⟧ (⟦P301⟧):⟦P302⟧⟦P303⟧⟦P304⟧⟦P305⟧,⟦P306⟧⟦P307⟧⟦P308⟧⟦P309⟧⟦P310⟧Excluded Reports⟦P311⟧The reports in the Go vulnerability database are collected from different⟦P312⟧sources and curated by the Go Security team. We may come across a vulnerability advisory⟦P313⟧(for example, a ⟦P314⟧ or ⟦P315⟧) and choose to exclude it for a variety of reasons.⟦P316⟧In these cases, a minimal report will be created in the ⟦P317⟧ repository,⟦P318⟧under⟦P319⟧⟦P320⟧⟦P321⟧⟦P322⟧.⟦P323⟧Reports may be excluded for these reasons:⟦P324⟧⟦P325⟧: The vulnerability is not in a Go package,⟦P326⟧but it was marked as a security advisory for the Go ecosystem by another source.⟦P327⟧This vulnerability cannot affect any⟦P328⟧Go packages. (For example, a vulnerability in  a ⟦P329⟧ library.)⟦P330⟧⟦P331⟧: The vulnerability occurs in package ⟦P332⟧, an ⟦P333⟧⟦P334⟧package only imported by package ⟦P335⟧, or some  other location which can⟦P336⟧never be imported by another module.⟦P337⟧⟦P338⟧: While the vulnerability occurs in a Go package which⟦P339⟧can be imported by another module, the package is not intended for external⟦P340⟧use and is not likely to ever be imported outside the module in which it is⟦P341⟧defined.⟦P342⟧⟦P343⟧: This vulnerability is a subset of another⟦P344⟧vulnerability in the database. For example, if package A contains a⟦P345⟧vulnerability, package B depends on package A, and there are separate ⟦P346⟧ IDs⟦P347⟧for packages A and B, we might mark the report for B as a dependent⟦P348⟧vulnerability entirely superseded by the report for A.⟦P349⟧⟦P350⟧: While a ⟦P351⟧ ⟦P352⟧ or ⟦P353⟧ has been assigned, there is no⟦P354⟧known vulnerability associated with it.⟦P355⟧⟦P356⟧: The vulnerability has been withdrawn by its source.⟦P357⟧At the moment, excluded reports are not served via⟦P358⟧⟦P359⟧⟦P360⟧⟦P361⟧ ⟦P362⟧. However, if you have⟦P363⟧a specific use case and it would be helpful to have access to this information⟦P364⟧through the ⟦P365⟧,⟦P366⟧⟦P367⟧please let us know⟦P368⟧.⟦P369⟧
